WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.441 --> 00:00:08.877
Welcome back. Your Grace.

2
00:00:08.977 --> 00:00:10.645
I take it you had a good flight?

3
00:00:10.745 --> 00:00:13.347
-I'm knackered.
-Mm.

4
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.518
-Has she started?
-Uh. she has.

5
00:00:16.618 --> 00:00:19.052
My new associate Deandra
will have your bags

6
00:00:19.152 --> 00:00:20.955
brought to your suite
while you're in the café.

7
00:00:21.021 --> 00:00:24.726
Ah. Of course.
We'll take care of it.

8
00:00:38.171 --> 00:00:41.676
Yes. taking a chance on love.
which is what you did

9
00:00:41.776 --> 00:00:44.478
when you made your reservation
six months ago.

10
00:00:44.546 --> 00:00:46.446
But it already seems worth it.
doesn't it?

11
00:00:46.548 --> 00:00:49.182
Seeing me up close.
in the flesh.

12
00:00:49.283 --> 00:00:51.553
an award-winning
cabaret artiste.

13
00:00:51.653 --> 00:00:53.922
-One award.
-Excuse me. Jolly?

14
00:00:54.054 --> 00:00:55.255
You've won one award.

15
00:00:55.389 --> 00:00:56.591
Most people hear
"award-winning."

16
00:00:56.724 --> 00:00:57.926
they think "several."

17
00:00:58.026 --> 00:01:00.895
Thanks for the clarification.
Bitch.

18
00:01:02.362 --> 00:01:03.531
It's true.

19
00:01:03.631 --> 00:01:05.299
I've won only one statue.

20
00:01:05.399 --> 00:01:07.835
and I happen to have it with me.
Do you want to see it?

21
00:01:07.936 --> 00:01:09.938
Yes.

22
00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.740
No. it's not the Emmy

23
00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.776
for my guest spot
on <i>Father Crime.</i>

24
00:01:14.909 --> 00:01:15.910
which airs tonight.

25
00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:18.345
This is my 1987

26
00:01:18.445 --> 00:01:21.448
Mabel Short Cabaret
Performer of the Year Award.

27
00:01:22.617 --> 00:01:24.251
Thank you.
Thank you.

28
00:01:24.351 --> 00:01:27.254
So. you are seeing
a legitimate

29
00:01:27.354 --> 00:01:29.089
cabaret performer. maybe

30
00:01:29.222 --> 00:01:30.825
the last one in existence.

31
00:01:30.925 --> 00:01:33.360
You know. the minute
they drag me out of here.

32
00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.631
some reality star will start
squawking Sondheim.

33
00:01:36.764 --> 00:01:40.068
So. let's make the most
of this night. shall we?

34
00:01:42.436 --> 00:01:43.771
Isn't she fantastic?

35
00:01:43.871 --> 00:01:46.106
Damn it!

36
00:01:46.206 --> 00:01:48.442
What is it?
What is it?

37
00:01:48.576 --> 00:01:49.711
No!

38
00:01:49.811 --> 00:01:51.378
Don't tell me.

39
00:01:52.714 --> 00:01:54.849
I love it
when she gets like this.

40
00:01:55.617 --> 00:01:57.852
Oh. And the tempo
on "Sunny Side of the Street"

41
00:01:57.952 --> 00:01:59.319
was too bright.

42
00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:01.121
What part of "sunny side"
don't you understand?

43
00:02:01.221 --> 00:02:02.456
Don't get fresh.

44
00:02:02.557 --> 00:02:04.092
And don't correct me
in the middle of a show.

45
00:02:04.191 --> 00:02:06.326
You asked.

46
00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:09.329
Especially when I ask.

47
00:02:09.463 --> 00:02:11.131
It's no wonder I went up
with all the latecomers

48
00:02:11.264 --> 00:02:13.101
and schmoozers in here.

49
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.168
Nobody knows
the pressure I'm under.

50
00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:16.771
And I'm on the tube tonight.

51
00:02:16.838 --> 00:02:19.306
We heard.
You told everybody.

52
00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:20.642
I'm gonna watch upstairs.

53
00:02:20.742 --> 00:02:22.242
I'll be back down
for the second show.

54
00:02:22.342 --> 00:02:24.512
Have them send my dinner up
through room service.

55
00:02:25.278 --> 00:02:26.648
They charge for that. you know.

56
00:02:26.748 --> 00:02:28.382
What are you implying?

57
00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:35.023
Oh. right there's fine.

58
00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:38.993
I'm on <i>Father Crime</i> tonight.
Do you watch that show?

59
00:02:39.127 --> 00:02:41.829
-Not since they
killed off Felicity.
-Oh.

60
00:02:43.397 --> 00:02:44.498
Wow.

61
00:02:44.599 --> 00:02:46.534
You guys have
raised prices again.

62
00:02:47.535 --> 00:02:48.503
Put it on my tab.

63
00:02:48.636 --> 00:02:50.237
Oh. wait.
Here.

64
00:02:50.337 --> 00:02:53.273
Oh.
I don't have any cash.

65
00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:54.742
I'll get you next time.

66
00:03:04.819 --> 00:03:07.220
Want some company. guv'nor?

67
00:03:10.124 --> 00:03:13.761
I know why you're here.
and it's not going to work.

68
00:03:14.962 --> 00:03:17.497
I can be very persuasive.
you know.

69
00:03:17.565 --> 00:03:21.135
I have to protect
my Manhattan investments.

70
00:03:21.234 --> 00:03:22.402
The royal family

71
00:03:22.503 --> 00:03:25.006
is under tremendous scrutiny.

72
00:03:25.106 --> 00:03:27.041
We can't get away
with anything.

73
00:03:27.175 --> 00:03:28.176
Exactly.

74
00:03:28.241 --> 00:03:29.677
The deal smells.

75
00:03:29.744 --> 00:03:31.179
and if you agree to it.

76
00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:33.181
you'll be in big trouble
when everything goes south.

77
00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:35.650
I've made up my mind.

78
00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:38.218
This place is due for a change.

79
00:03:38.318 --> 00:03:39.687
Why does anything
have to change?

80
00:03:39.787 --> 00:03:41.055
Just give it a little more time.

81
00:03:41.155 --> 00:03:42.389
-I'll see you out.
-No. no.

82
00:03:42.489 --> 00:03:44.257
-no. no. no. no. no. no.
-Come on.

83
00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:45.727
Stop. now.
Stop!

84
00:03:45.827 --> 00:03:48.763
Good God. woman.

85
00:03:48.896 --> 00:03:50.798
Are you mad?

86
00:03:50.898 --> 00:03:53.101
Something like that.

87
00:04:26.801 --> 00:04:27.935
Yes.

88
00:04:28.035 --> 00:04:29.637
it's true.
I've won only one statue.

89
00:04:29.737 --> 00:04:31.404
and I happen to have it with me.

90
00:04:31.471 --> 00:04:33.708
- Do you want to see it?
- Yes!

91
00:04:33.808 --> 00:04:35.543
Let's see it!

92
00:04:35.643 --> 00:04:37.310
Whip it out!

93
00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:40.447
You know what?
I don't do this for the awards.

94
00:04:40.548 --> 00:04:42.183
I do it for the money.

95
00:04:42.282 --> 00:04:43.885
So I'll just keep singing.

96
00:04:59.901 --> 00:05:01.903
Oh.

97
00:05:01.969 --> 00:05:05.006
Hello. I'm looking
for the. um... Oh.

98
00:05:05.106 --> 00:05:07.474
Wow. this place is so elegant.

99
00:05:07.608 --> 00:05:10.645
Uh. sorry.
I'm here for the. um...

100
00:05:10.745 --> 00:05:12.814
-Possible crime scene?
-Oh. Yes.

101
00:05:12.947 --> 00:05:15.382
- That's all right. I got this.
- Captain Wagner.

102
00:05:15.482 --> 00:05:17.084
I was expecting
to see a detective.

103
00:05:17.151 --> 00:05:20.154
Uh. since I got passed over
for commissioner.

104
00:05:20.254 --> 00:05:21.923
I figured I'd get out
of the office a bit.

105
00:05:22.023 --> 00:05:24.457
Oh. it's a beautiful place
for an investigation.

106
00:05:24.525 --> 00:05:27.394
-Thanks.
-The Reilly is
a New York institution.

107
00:05:27.494 --> 00:05:29.530
This place still looks the same
as the night

108
00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:32.499
I proposed to Claudia.
right there in the café.

109
00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:34.302
No wonder you're
in such a good mood.

110
00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:36.103
Hey. you should be. too.
given the news.

111
00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:37.538
What news?

112
00:05:37.638 --> 00:05:40.007
"Mayor Bloom. seen last night

113
00:05:40.107 --> 00:05:42.475
having dinner with
a mysterious brunette."

114
00:05:42.577 --> 00:05:45.880
That's not a phony story
planted by Nadine.

115
00:05:46.647 --> 00:05:50.751
The "mysterious redhead"
is officially yesterday's news.

116
00:05:50.852 --> 00:05:53.588
You must be relieved.

117
00:05:53.688 --> 00:05:56.991
Yeah. Absolutely.

118
00:05:57.058 --> 00:05:58.259
Yay.

119
00:05:59.026 --> 00:06:01.729
Housekeeping found him
this morning.

120
00:06:01.863 --> 00:06:04.699
It looks like he slipped
getting out of the shower.

121
00:06:04.832 --> 00:06:08.135
Apparently. he grabbed the towel
bar. but it came off the wall.

122
00:06:08.202 --> 00:06:10.905
leaving him to smash his head
on the tile.

123
00:06:11.005 --> 00:06:13.274
Oh. please. sir.
don't touch anything.

124
00:06:13.373 --> 00:06:16.744
Oh. right. Sorry.
Force of habit.

125
00:06:16.844 --> 00:06:18.312
Uh. what were you about to do?

126
00:06:18.411 --> 00:06:21.215
Oh. we like the seams on
our lampshades to face the wall.

127
00:06:21.349 --> 00:06:23.383
Excuse me. sir.

128
00:06:23.483 --> 00:06:24.852
Uh. e-excuse me.

129
00:06:24.919 --> 00:06:26.120
Oh.

130
00:06:28.022 --> 00:06:29.757
Whoa.

131
00:06:29.891 --> 00:06:32.526
Everything is just so.

132
00:06:32.593 --> 00:06:35.495
with the hotel monogram
on everything.

133
00:06:35.563 --> 00:06:38.165
Such a lovely old-world touch.

134
00:06:38.266 --> 00:06:40.067
It's like stepping back in time.

135
00:06:40.167 --> 00:06:42.503
Yeah. wow.
It's like he lived here.

136
00:06:42.603 --> 00:06:44.071
Oh. he did.

137
00:06:44.205 --> 00:06:46.674
Part of the time he kept
this place as a pied-à-terre.

138
00:06:46.774 --> 00:06:48.743
Oh. that's right.
I forget that some people

139
00:06:48.876 --> 00:06:51.045
-actually live in hotels.
-Mm-hmm.

140
00:06:51.145 --> 00:06:54.749
Huh. These pajamas are folded.

141
00:06:54.882 --> 00:06:56.751
and these are on a hanger.

142
00:06:56.884 --> 00:07:00.087
-Well. maybe he was planning
on wearing those.
-Ooh.

143
00:07:00.187 --> 00:07:01.555
Is that blood?

144
00:07:01.622 --> 00:07:06.127
Maybe from a scuffle
that knocked over the lamp?

145
00:07:06.227 --> 00:07:08.095
Or it could be from shaving.

146
00:07:09.096 --> 00:07:13.501
Oh. This shoehorn is kind of
bent out of shape.

147
00:07:13.601 --> 00:07:16.170
Nobody has that much trouble
putting on their loafers.

148
00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:19.941
-Yeah. not usually.
-Hmm.

149
00:07:21.409 --> 00:07:22.376
It's just in here.

150
00:07:22.442 --> 00:07:23.644
What was that about?

151
00:07:23.778 --> 00:07:26.446
Oh. Roger came to draw
the Duke's bath.

152
00:07:26.547 --> 00:07:31.152
They had an arrangement for him
to do so whenever he was here.

153
00:07:31.285 --> 00:07:33.087
He took morning baths?

154
00:07:33.154 --> 00:07:36.290
Then why was he taking
a nighttime shower?

155
00:07:37.058 --> 00:07:39.060
We'll need to get
as much information as we can

156
00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:40.061
from your staff.

157
00:07:40.127 --> 00:07:41.929
You said that the towel bar

158
00:07:41.996 --> 00:07:44.665
just came off the wall?

159
00:07:44.799 --> 00:07:46.667
Has anything like that
ever happened

160
00:07:46.767 --> 00:07:49.937
-at the Hotel Reilly before?
-Well. not recently.

161
00:07:50.037 --> 00:07:52.039
We've had some incidents
of wear and tear.

162
00:07:52.139 --> 00:07:55.509
but that's why
we've begun renovations.

163
00:07:55.609 --> 00:07:57.979
including this very suite.

164
00:07:58.112 --> 00:07:59.981
This bathroom was remodeled
six months ago.

165
00:08:00.081 --> 00:08:03.951
So it's very possible
that somebody

166
00:08:04.018 --> 00:08:06.454
broke it off on purpose

167
00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:09.757
and made it look
like an accident.

168
00:08:09.857 --> 00:08:11.726
But how?

169
00:08:15.162 --> 00:08:17.231
I've never seen
anything like this.

170
00:08:17.331 --> 00:08:19.100
and I've worked at the Reilly
for 29 years.

171
00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:21.268
Oh. you must have
so many stories.

172
00:08:21.369 --> 00:08:22.670
Oh. we all do.

173
00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:24.538
Uh. but we're discreet.

174
00:08:24.672 --> 00:08:27.308
which is exactly what I told
Julia Roberts and Ted Kennedy.

175
00:08:27.375 --> 00:08:29.677
-We appreciate it.
-Well. let me know
whatever you need.

176
00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:32.146
and. um. uh.
if you can't reach me.

177
00:08:32.246 --> 00:08:35.016
you can speak
to my associate Deandra.

178
00:08:36.283 --> 00:08:38.452
Hello.

179
00:08:38.552 --> 00:08:40.688
De-- Uh. what did you say
your name was again?

180
00:08:40.788 --> 00:08:44.125
Um. because you remind me
of a woman I once met

181
00:08:44.225 --> 00:08:46.160
named Denise Jackson.

182
00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:48.562
Well. I'm Deandra Jordan.

183
00:08:48.696 --> 00:08:51.699
Any questions you have.
come to me.

184
00:08:51.799 --> 00:08:53.567
In the meantime.

185
00:08:53.667 --> 00:08:56.570
if you'd like
some complimentary coffee. tea.

186
00:08:56.704 --> 00:09:00.041
or juice. please help yourself.

187
00:09:00.174 --> 00:09:02.543
Thank you.

188
00:09:03.411 --> 00:09:04.779
Who's that?

189
00:09:06.047 --> 00:09:07.882
It's Ruby Lane.

190
00:09:08.015 --> 00:09:10.451
She performs in the café.
lives upstairs.

191
00:09:10.551 --> 00:09:13.921
Wait. <i>the</i> Ruby Lane?
She's still around?

192
00:09:14.055 --> 00:09:15.856
You're out of stevia.

193
00:09:15.923 --> 00:09:18.559
That should not happen
at a decent hotel.

194
00:09:18.692 --> 00:09:22.463
I apologize.
It's been a difficult morning.

195
00:09:22.563 --> 00:09:25.433
Yes. uh. one of the residents.
Duke Sebastian.

196
00:09:25.566 --> 00:09:28.402
slipped in the shower and died.

197
00:09:28.503 --> 00:09:30.271
My God. How tragic.

198
00:09:32.473 --> 00:09:33.941
No surprise.

199
00:09:34.041 --> 00:09:36.577
I'll go let the kitchen know
about the stevia shortage.

200
00:09:36.677 --> 00:09:39.680
This whole place
is falling apart.

201
00:09:39.747 --> 00:09:42.316
Something like this
was bound to happen again.

202
00:09:42.416 --> 00:09:44.919
I just said he slipped
in the shower.

203
00:09:44.985 --> 00:09:47.888
not that there was
any physical damage.

204
00:09:47.955 --> 00:09:50.591
Mm. I assumed.

205
00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:54.228
It's all breaking down.

206
00:09:54.328 --> 00:09:56.430
My room-- you have
to jiggle the handle.

207
00:09:56.531 --> 00:09:59.300
You two should come
and see my show.

208
00:09:59.433 --> 00:10:01.769
If you can afford the cover
charge and the two-drink minimum

209
00:10:01.902 --> 00:10:03.270
on a cop's salary.

210
00:10:03.404 --> 00:10:04.805
I'm a captain.

211
00:10:04.939 --> 00:10:06.107
Yeah. but I'll save up.

212
00:10:07.808 --> 00:10:10.444
Oh. hey. I have that very tote.

213
00:10:10.545 --> 00:10:12.379
Well. somewhere
in my collection.

214
00:10:21.388 --> 00:10:22.690
Bye.

215
00:10:24.291 --> 00:10:27.795
Oh. you asked for more
information from housekeeping.

216
00:10:27.895 --> 00:10:30.599
It seems some of the more
expensive items were missing

217
00:10:30.698 --> 00:10:31.832
from the Duke's minibar.

218
00:10:31.966 --> 00:10:33.968
including
the yogurt-covered raisins.

219
00:10:34.068 --> 00:10:35.302
Ooh. I love those.

220
00:10:35.436 --> 00:10:38.239
But there was no sign of them
in the trash.

221
00:10:38.305 --> 00:10:39.940
Well. who would murder someone
and walk off

222
00:10:40.007 --> 00:10:42.643
with the goodies
from the minibar?

223
00:10:42.743 --> 00:10:44.178
Hmm.

224
00:11:00.027 --> 00:11:01.829
Oh. my God.
it's like you're in every story.

225
00:11:01.929 --> 00:11:04.098
Teddy. What are you doing here?

226
00:11:04.165 --> 00:11:07.101
Well. um. I have some news.

227
00:11:07.168 --> 00:11:08.637
Maybe. I think so.

228
00:11:08.702 --> 00:11:10.037
But if you're reading
about Alec's new girlfriend.

229
00:11:10.137 --> 00:11:12.039
-it can wait.
-Oh. uh...

230
00:11:12.173 --> 00:11:15.309
Whoever she is.
she has my sympathy.

231
00:11:15.376 --> 00:11:17.044
-You sure?
-Positive.

232
00:11:17.144 --> 00:11:21.516
So. um. what is your news.
maybe. you think so?

233
00:11:21.616 --> 00:11:26.153
Well. um. Roy and I
may be getting married.

234
00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:27.188
What?

235
00:11:27.288 --> 00:11:28.355
-Teddy.
-Yeah.

236
00:11:28.455 --> 00:11:30.291
- Oh. I'm so happy.
- Ah!

237
00:11:30.357 --> 00:11:32.927
- Well. I said maybe. Um...
- Okay.

238
00:11:33.027 --> 00:11:34.929
But we did have a conversation

239
00:11:35.029 --> 00:11:36.030
that felt like it.

240
00:11:36.163 --> 00:11:37.532
though we changed subjects

241
00:11:37.632 --> 00:11:39.733
-because I started
hyperventilating.
-Oh.

242
00:11:39.867 --> 00:11:41.368
Well. I am thrilled.

243
00:11:41.503 --> 00:11:44.205
to the extent
that it's more than maybe.

244
00:11:44.305 --> 00:11:47.007
Yeah. but the thing is.
I'm pretty sure Roy wants me

245
00:11:47.074 --> 00:11:49.544
to propose because
I'm basically allergic

246
00:11:49.644 --> 00:11:51.680
-to overt romantic gestures.
-That is true.

247
00:11:51.745 --> 00:11:53.948
but have no fear.
I have so many ideas.

248
00:11:54.048 --> 00:11:56.317
-Uh...
-Okay. Did you know

249
00:11:56.417 --> 00:11:58.786
that you can order skywriting
over Times Square?

250
00:11:58.919 --> 00:12:02.756
Yeah. no. Um. I was thinking
something lower-key. Like. um.

251
00:12:02.890 --> 00:12:05.392
during the finale of a show
that we're watching on Thursday.

252
00:12:05.492 --> 00:12:08.195
sharing a frozen Hawaiian pizza
with a thrift store ring

253
00:12:08.262 --> 00:12:10.264
hidden under a piece
of Canadian bacon.

254
00:12:11.365 --> 00:12:14.703
Sometimes I forget that.
in some ways.

255
00:12:14.802 --> 00:12:16.237
you do take after your father.

256
00:12:16.337 --> 00:12:20.074
who proposed to me
in a footnote in a legal brief.

257
00:12:20.174 --> 00:12:21.842
Okay. um...

258
00:12:21.942 --> 00:12:24.512
Well. what-what if I cooked for
him. just the way you taught me?

259
00:12:24.579 --> 00:12:26.280
Yeah... what else you got?

260
00:12:26.413 --> 00:12:28.949
You know what? It's gonna be
fine. even without your help.

261
00:12:29.083 --> 00:12:31.720
So I will. uh.
let you know how it goes.

262
00:12:31.819 --> 00:12:33.787
-Oh.
-Oh. God.

263
00:12:33.887 --> 00:12:36.023
We're really
gonna do this. maybe.

264
00:12:46.668 --> 00:12:49.770
And this is our business center.

265
00:12:49.870 --> 00:12:51.272
We can talk in here.

266
00:12:51.372 --> 00:12:53.874
-Why in the business center?
-Oh. nobody ever uses it.

267
00:12:53.941 --> 00:12:56.477
- Oh.
- So. start from the beginning.

268
00:12:56.578 --> 00:12:59.880
Right. This hotel has been
engaged in shady bookkeeping

269
00:12:59.980 --> 00:13:03.317
and tax fraud since it was built
during Prohibition.

270
00:13:03.417 --> 00:13:04.619
Wow. that is the beginning.

271
00:13:04.753 --> 00:13:06.621
And with Alec Bloom
as the new mayor

272
00:13:06.721 --> 00:13:08.989
promising greater scrutiny.

273
00:13:09.123 --> 00:13:13.394
the current owner
Havenford Realty Group--

274
00:13:13.494 --> 00:13:14.629
they want out.

275
00:13:14.729 --> 00:13:17.164
But any sale depends on approval

276
00:13:17.264 --> 00:13:18.966
from the long-term residents.

277
00:13:19.066 --> 00:13:22.436
The Duke came back to New York
to cast his vote.

278
00:13:22.504 --> 00:13:26.140
but after what happened.
the vote has been postponed.

279
00:13:26.273 --> 00:13:27.776
Any idea who the buyer is?

280
00:13:27.841 --> 00:13:29.376
Havenford isn't saying.

281
00:13:29.476 --> 00:13:32.681
It's part of my mission
to find out. but my guess is.

282
00:13:32.781 --> 00:13:35.650
it's one of the mysterious
guests staying here this week.

283
00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:39.086
<i>I've spotted a mob boss.</i>
<i>a Saudi sheik.</i>

284
00:13:39.186 --> 00:13:41.855
<i>and a Russian oligarch.</i>
<i>but I can't be sure.</i>

285
00:13:41.955 --> 00:13:43.223
And you think the Duke
could have been murdered

286
00:13:43.324 --> 00:13:44.858
-because of his vote?
-I don't know.

287
00:13:44.958 --> 00:13:47.562
but the timing is suspicious.

288
00:13:47.662 --> 00:13:49.963
Ruby Lane would have a vote.
She lives here.

289
00:13:50.064 --> 00:13:52.299
Could she be involved
in those financial schemes?

290
00:13:52.399 --> 00:13:55.035
You mean the lady
who steals two gallons

291
00:13:55.169 --> 00:13:56.538
of lobby coffee every morning

292
00:13:56.638 --> 00:13:58.540
and who can't remember
her lyrics?

293
00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:00.140
-I doubt it.
-All right.

294
00:14:00.207 --> 00:14:02.476
I think I'll have some lunch
in the hotel lobby bar

295
00:14:02.544 --> 00:14:04.011
and interview the guests.

296
00:14:04.144 --> 00:14:05.279
Are you sure
you're not just trying

297
00:14:05.346 --> 00:14:07.114
to soak up the atmosphere?

298
00:14:07.181 --> 00:14:09.850
I can do both.

299
00:14:09.950 --> 00:14:12.086
Uh. hey. do you happen
to know what floor

300
00:14:12.219 --> 00:14:13.788
Ruby Lane lives on?

301
00:14:21.028 --> 00:14:22.797
-You have a good eye.
-Oh.

302
00:14:22.896 --> 00:14:24.865
I don't know about that.

303
00:14:24.965 --> 00:14:26.735
I just like beautiful things.

304
00:14:26.867 --> 00:14:28.035
And dogs.

305
00:14:28.168 --> 00:14:29.870
The exquisite Reilly murals

306
00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:32.507
once belonged to the Blick
family of the Blick Museum.

307
00:14:32.574 --> 00:14:34.743
I happen to be very close
to several Blicks.

308
00:14:34.875 --> 00:14:36.644
Montgomery Blakemont III.

309
00:14:36.745 --> 00:14:39.012
art connoisseur and
philanthropist. Call me Monty.

310
00:14:39.079 --> 00:14:41.482
Hi. Monty.
Elsbeth Tascioni.

311
00:14:41.549 --> 00:14:44.017
Consent decree lawyer
with the NYPD.

312
00:14:44.084 --> 00:14:46.186
I'm talking art appreciation
with the fuzz.

313
00:14:46.286 --> 00:14:47.522
How fantastic is that?

314
00:14:47.622 --> 00:14:49.089
Pretty fantastic.

315
00:14:49.223 --> 00:14:50.324
This hotel is filled
with great art.

316
00:14:50.391 --> 00:14:51.693
but not all of it
in public spaces.

317
00:14:51.793 --> 00:14:52.993
There are treasures to be found

318
00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:54.395
-in certain suites.
-Wow.

319
00:14:54.495 --> 00:14:56.930
-You seem to know so much.
-I do.

320
00:14:57.030 --> 00:14:58.399
-I keep a place here.
-Oh.

321
00:14:58.499 --> 00:15:00.100
Were you here
when the Duke died?

322
00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:02.871
Oh. yes. I was in the café.

323
00:15:02.936 --> 00:15:04.304
-Oh.
-Uh. in the first show.

324
00:15:04.405 --> 00:15:06.273
Ruby forgot her lyrics.
it was epic.

325
00:15:06.407 --> 00:15:09.209
And then. at
the late show. she was on fire.

326
00:15:09.276 --> 00:15:11.646
You went to the café
twice in one night?

327
00:15:11.746 --> 00:15:14.181
-Mm.
-That's a four-drink minimum.

328
00:15:14.281 --> 00:15:15.949
Isn't that expensive?

329
00:15:17.384 --> 00:15:20.454
Excuse me. Ah.
It's the Metropolitan Museum.

330
00:15:20.588 --> 00:15:22.524
I'm gifting them $20 million.
and we're arguing

331
00:15:22.624 --> 00:15:24.124
over the size
of my name on the plaque.

332
00:15:24.258 --> 00:15:25.860
-Can you imagine?
-No.

333
00:15:25.959 --> 00:15:29.062
This is Monty. Are you ready
to talk some sense?

334
00:15:29.764 --> 00:15:31.465
Okay. great.

335
00:15:31.566 --> 00:15:33.635
Thank you.

336
00:15:34.669 --> 00:15:36.805
Oh. hello again.

337
00:15:36.937 --> 00:15:38.606
-Aren't you getting in?
-Oh. no.

338
00:15:38.706 --> 00:15:40.407
Uh. actually.
I-I changed my mind.

339
00:15:40.474 --> 00:15:43.812
Oh. Well. suit yourself.
Excuse me. I have to rehearse.

340
00:15:43.944 --> 00:15:46.413
You still need to rehearse
after all these years?

341
00:15:46.514 --> 00:15:48.917
Well. I don't need to.
but I dropped something

342
00:15:48.982 --> 00:15:50.718
the other night.
and Jolly. my accompanist.

343
00:15:50.785 --> 00:15:52.152
got his knickers in a twist.

344
00:15:52.252 --> 00:15:55.322
Oh.
Um. is there any chance

345
00:15:55.456 --> 00:15:58.258
-that I could sit in and watch?
-No.

346
00:15:58.325 --> 00:16:01.796
Oh. please?
I-I-It would be so fascinating

347
00:16:01.930 --> 00:16:04.331
to watch a legend at work.

348
00:16:04.431 --> 00:16:05.633
Do you go to nightclubs a lot?

349
00:16:05.733 --> 00:16:07.067
Uh. no. that's just the thing.

350
00:16:07.134 --> 00:16:09.904
I know so little about cabaret
as an art form.

351
00:16:10.003 --> 00:16:12.272
Um. is it anything like <i>Glee?</i>

352
00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:14.843
Are you for real?

353
00:16:14.975 --> 00:16:18.813
You mean am I... serious?
Absolutely.

354
00:16:18.913 --> 00:16:22.082
Although... I am really here
with the police.

355
00:16:22.182 --> 00:16:24.752
helping them
with their murder investigation.

356
00:16:37.498 --> 00:16:40.234
Yes. taking a chance on love.
which is what you did

357
00:16:40.334 --> 00:16:42.804
when you made your reservation
six months ago.

358
00:16:42.871 --> 00:16:44.706
Blah. blah. blah.
We do that whole bit.

359
00:16:44.839 --> 00:16:46.340
Oh. so we're not
just winging it?

360
00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:49.176
I forgot. I said I was sorry.

361
00:16:50.344 --> 00:16:52.580
Hey. Little Orphan Annie.
what do you think so far?

362
00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:53.948
You're incredible.

363
00:16:54.014 --> 00:16:58.485
You make every word
so... specific.

364
00:16:58.553 --> 00:17:02.022
It's like. uh. it's like
you're singing just to me.

365
00:17:02.155 --> 00:17:03.791
Well. I mean. uh.
you are right now.

366
00:17:03.858 --> 00:17:08.061
but it's like...
you're speaking to my soul.

367
00:17:08.195 --> 00:17:09.964
She gets it.

368
00:17:10.063 --> 00:17:13.902
Cabaret is the most intimate
form of communication.

369
00:17:14.034 --> 00:17:17.005
Up here.
there's nowhere to hide.

370
00:17:17.070 --> 00:17:19.908
The lights. the proximity
expose everything.

371
00:17:20.008 --> 00:17:22.744
Believe me. what I do...

372
00:17:22.877 --> 00:17:25.145
is not about a beautiful voice.

373
00:17:25.212 --> 00:17:26.915
Oh. we believe you.

374
00:17:28.048 --> 00:17:32.185
- Ignore him. The way I do
when he falls down in a gutter.

375
00:17:32.286 --> 00:17:33.955
You two are so funny.

376
00:17:34.054 --> 00:17:35.890
How long have you two
been working together?

377
00:17:36.024 --> 00:17:39.894
Let's see. next month
it will have been an eternity.

378
00:17:39.994 --> 00:17:42.062
- But it's worth it
to show up every night

379
00:17:42.195 --> 00:17:43.865
and dream of getting paid.

380
00:17:43.932 --> 00:17:45.499
Including two shows last night.

381
00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:47.735
-As usual.
-What do you do between shows?

382
00:17:47.835 --> 00:17:50.572
-I have dinner in here.
-Except the night before last.

383
00:17:50.672 --> 00:17:52.874
You mean the night
the Duke died?

384
00:17:52.974 --> 00:17:57.779
Oh. right. That night I wanted
to watch myself on the tube.

385
00:17:57.879 --> 00:18:01.448
I recently shot a guest spot
on <i>Father Crime.</i>

386
00:18:01.583 --> 00:18:03.618
Oh. I know that show.

387
00:18:03.718 --> 00:18:05.587
Wow. you must be
a great actress.

388
00:18:05.687 --> 00:18:08.056
<i>Father Crime</i> gets
the best guest stars.

389
00:18:08.155 --> 00:18:10.123
probably because
it shoots in New York.

390
00:18:10.257 --> 00:18:13.695
I was great.
and I had room service.

391
00:18:13.761 --> 00:18:17.865
Speaking of food. you probably
need lunch. don't you?

392
00:18:17.932 --> 00:18:21.069
Cherie. bring this lovely lady
a Waldorf salad.

393
00:18:21.168 --> 00:18:23.203
and bring one for me
while you're at it.

394
00:18:23.270 --> 00:18:26.106
-Thank you. Cherie. but...
-It's the best thing they do.
You'll love it.

395
00:18:26.239 --> 00:18:28.810
And you'll need your strength.
investigating that...

396
00:18:30.044 --> 00:18:32.547
-Did you say it was
a murder investigation?
-Mm-hmm.

397
00:18:32.614 --> 00:18:35.482
Duke Sebastian Mason.
Lord of Sussexford.

398
00:18:35.583 --> 00:18:36.784
Did-did you know him?

399
00:18:39.621 --> 00:18:43.658
No. but I heard he was
at my show the night he died.

400
00:18:44.224 --> 00:18:46.259
-Not a bad way to go.
-I'll say.

401
00:18:46.326 --> 00:18:48.963
Especially if you made
the reservation

402
00:18:49.063 --> 00:18:50.598
six months earlier.

403
00:18:50.665 --> 00:18:53.768
You have a cute personality.
Do you sing?

404
00:18:53.835 --> 00:18:56.004
Me? Oh. gosh. no. no.

405
00:18:56.136 --> 00:18:58.673
But. um. my son Teddy--
he has a beautiful voice.

406
00:18:58.806 --> 00:19:01.509
He could be on Broadway.
but he's too shy.

407
00:19:01.609 --> 00:19:04.812
He recently told me
he may be getting married.

408
00:19:04.912 --> 00:19:06.480
-Really? Congratulations.
-Thanks.

409
00:19:06.614 --> 00:19:09.316
To be honest.
I'm a little nervous about it.

410
00:19:10.250 --> 00:19:12.419
Yeah. I'm afraid
he-he might pull away.

411
00:19:12.486 --> 00:19:16.323
I'm wondering. you know.
how I'll handle that.

412
00:20:35.570 --> 00:20:37.171
Oh. I love that song.

413
00:20:37.270 --> 00:20:41.075
I think that's my favorite song
by Irving Berlin.

414
00:20:42.143 --> 00:20:44.946
I thought you knew nothing
about cabaret.

415
00:20:45.780 --> 00:20:47.215
Oh. I don't.

416
00:20:47.314 --> 00:20:49.382
But I know
the Great American Songbook.

417
00:20:49.483 --> 00:20:51.853
I'm not a monster.

418
00:20:52.587 --> 00:20:56.356
I don't like your act. sister.
It's phony.

419
00:20:56.456 --> 00:20:59.961
Cherie. send my lunch upstairs.

420
00:21:00.061 --> 00:21:02.764
and charge it to her.

421
00:21:02.864 --> 00:21:06.299
You've got a lot of nerve.
Elsbeth.

422
00:21:08.569 --> 00:21:11.471
With all the things in the world
to get worked up about...

423
00:21:11.606 --> 00:21:13.340
Is it the drinking?

424
00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:16.944
I noticed she has a glass
in her hand in all the photos.

425
00:21:17.044 --> 00:21:20.047
That's just a prop to give her
a false air of sophistication.

426
00:21:20.114 --> 00:21:23.184
Hers is always filled
with water. unlike mine.

427
00:21:23.283 --> 00:21:25.853
So. when she forgets
her lyrics...

428
00:21:25.953 --> 00:21:28.890
Ms. Tascioni. come quick.
- Oh.

429
00:21:28.990 --> 00:21:30.290
What is it?

430
00:21:30.390 --> 00:21:32.325
Housekeeping heard someone
inside the Duke's suite.

431
00:21:32.459 --> 00:21:35.196
<i>So you think the killer returned</i>
<i>to the scene of the crime?</i>

432
00:21:35.295 --> 00:21:37.330
Whoever's in there. open up.

433
00:21:37.430 --> 00:21:39.466
This is the police.

434
00:21:46.674 --> 00:21:48.509
Oh. hey. you.

435
00:21:53.147 --> 00:21:55.583
Mr. Blakemont. we have learned
from someone at the hotel

436
00:21:55.650 --> 00:21:59.520
that your name doesn't appear
on any suite here at the hotel.

437
00:21:59.654 --> 00:22:01.789
Was it that new woman Deandra?
She seems on the ball.

438
00:22:01.889 --> 00:22:03.423
Why are you hanging
around the hotel?

439
00:22:03.524 --> 00:22:05.793
All right. if you must know.
I'm staying in a suite

440
00:22:05.893 --> 00:22:07.762
that's owned by a former boss

441
00:22:07.862 --> 00:22:10.198
but only while my townhouse
is being renovated.

442
00:22:10.298 --> 00:22:11.666
And does your former boss know?

443
00:22:11.766 --> 00:22:15.670
Of course he does. technically.
But don't worry.

444
00:22:15.770 --> 00:22:17.772
all of my employers
trust me implicitly.

445
00:22:17.839 --> 00:22:19.707
That's why they give me
power of attorney.

446
00:22:19.841 --> 00:22:21.676
But what were you doing here
in the Duke's suite?

447
00:22:21.776 --> 00:22:23.044
I was looking at the artwork.

448
00:22:23.177 --> 00:22:24.512
In a dead man's room?

449
00:22:24.612 --> 00:22:26.781
As I told Elsbeth.
not all of the art at the Reilly

450
00:22:26.881 --> 00:22:28.883
is publicly displayed.
This room

451
00:22:29.016 --> 00:22:30.417
has an Edward Hopper

452
00:22:30.518 --> 00:22:32.253
plus a tiny Titian.

453
00:22:33.020 --> 00:22:34.555
It's true. He did tell me that.

454
00:22:34.689 --> 00:22:36.356
Well. you're still a suspect.

455
00:22:36.456 --> 00:22:39.359
And since you're such an expert
on the Hotel Reilly.

456
00:22:39.459 --> 00:22:42.697
what do you know about that vote
to approve a sale?

457
00:22:42.797 --> 00:22:45.800
I know everything about it. My
former boss made me his proxy.

458
00:22:45.900 --> 00:22:47.168
Of course he did.

459
00:22:47.235 --> 00:22:48.536
Which way do you think
it would have gone?

460
00:22:48.636 --> 00:22:50.872
Oh. it was split
right down the middle.

461
00:22:51.005 --> 00:22:53.373
The in-favor side insisted
the Duke come for the vote

462
00:22:53.507 --> 00:22:54.407
to put them over the top.

463
00:22:54.542 --> 00:22:55.810
It was very exciting.

464
00:22:55.877 --> 00:22:57.678
-Why? Why did it matter so much?
-Well.

465
00:22:57.745 --> 00:22:59.647
rumor is. the mystery buyer
planned a major.

466
00:22:59.714 --> 00:23:02.049
very tasteful renovation.
but to pay for it.

467
00:23:02.149 --> 00:23:03.551
they were gonna have
to clean up the books.

468
00:23:03.651 --> 00:23:05.052
so that meant that
the long-term residents

469
00:23:05.152 --> 00:23:06.854
would have to settle up
their maintenance.

470
00:23:06.921 --> 00:23:09.456
rents. bar tabs.
Some of these people

471
00:23:09.557 --> 00:23:10.691
have been getting away
with murder

472
00:23:10.791 --> 00:23:12.126
while guzzling the free coffee.

473
00:23:12.226 --> 00:23:14.161
-Mm.
-And what about you?

474
00:23:14.228 --> 00:23:15.663
Eh... how were you gonna vote?

475
00:23:15.730 --> 00:23:17.632
I was in favor. all the way.

476
00:23:17.765 --> 00:23:19.200
I encouraged the others.

477
00:23:19.300 --> 00:23:21.369
Here. let me see my phone.
I'll show you.

478
00:23:23.704 --> 00:23:26.507
I have a thing
about freeloaders.

479
00:23:26.607 --> 00:23:28.276
By the way.
the hotel Wi-Fi is pretty weak.

480
00:23:28.408 --> 00:23:31.444
but I have the code
to the Sephora next door.

481
00:23:32.713 --> 00:23:36.250
Roy. To what
do I owe the pleasure?

482
00:23:36.350 --> 00:23:37.718
I have some news. Well. maybe.

483
00:23:37.785 --> 00:23:41.188
Teddy and I
may be getting married.

484
00:23:41.289 --> 00:23:45.359
Oh. that is so great.
I am so happy.

485
00:23:45.458 --> 00:23:47.762
I mean. maybe.
We had a conversation about it.

486
00:23:47.828 --> 00:23:50.331
but then Teddy
started hyperventilating.

487
00:23:50.463 --> 00:23:51.766
Oh. yeah. he does that.

488
00:23:53.267 --> 00:23:55.468
But before anything happens.

489
00:23:55.569 --> 00:23:58.673
I wanted to come
and ask your permission.

490
00:23:58.806 --> 00:24:02.243
Oh. Roy. Of course.

491
00:24:02.343 --> 00:24:03.945
- Yes.
- Okay.

492
00:24:04.045 --> 00:24:06.013
I didn't know
you were so traditional.

493
00:24:06.147 --> 00:24:09.150
That's why I want
a super romantic proposal scene.

494
00:24:09.250 --> 00:24:10.851
and we both know
Teddy will never.

495
00:24:10.985 --> 00:24:12.320
-Mm.
-What can I say?

496
00:24:12.420 --> 00:24:14.188
I'm old-fashioned.
brought up in the suburbs.

497
00:24:14.288 --> 00:24:16.223
repressed but very performative.

498
00:24:16.324 --> 00:24:18.491
Teddy better get used to it.

499
00:24:18.626 --> 00:24:19.994
Uh. wait.

500
00:24:20.094 --> 00:24:21.529
Does that mean
that you and Teddy

501
00:24:21.662 --> 00:24:24.198
might move to the suburbs
eventually?

502
00:24:24.298 --> 00:24:27.935
Probably. I mean. I love
New York. but I want kids.

503
00:24:28.035 --> 00:24:30.204
Oh.

504
00:24:32.974 --> 00:24:36.978
Okay. I still think
she's too much of a train wreck

505
00:24:37.044 --> 00:24:38.512
to kill anybody.

506
00:24:38.612 --> 00:24:41.215
but I did some digging
into Ruby's account.

507
00:24:41.315 --> 00:24:43.250
It's true. she is in arrears.

508
00:24:43.351 --> 00:24:47.221
but my friend in bookkeeping
hasn't told me how much yet.

509
00:24:47.321 --> 00:24:49.557
Oh. there you are. Deandra.

510
00:24:49.690 --> 00:24:51.859
-I need you.
-Coming.

511
00:24:52.693 --> 00:24:54.729
-Excuse me.
-Uh-huh.

512
00:24:55.730 --> 00:24:58.065
Now can we do a more formal
interview with her?

513
00:24:58.165 --> 00:25:01.135
I suppose. And it might be
interesting to see her suite.

514
00:25:01.202 --> 00:25:03.404
Ah. I bet it's pretty glamorous.

515
00:25:03.504 --> 00:25:05.306
Oh. sorry. it's such a mess.

516
00:25:05.373 --> 00:25:08.209
If I'd known you were coming.
I would have been out.

517
00:25:08.342 --> 00:25:11.212
-How long have you lived here?
-Who knows?

518
00:25:11.312 --> 00:25:14.382
I paid $28.000 for it.
if it gives you an idea.

519
00:25:14.482 --> 00:25:15.649
Wow.

520
00:25:15.716 --> 00:25:17.184
And how do you feel
about the possible sale?

521
00:25:17.251 --> 00:25:20.054
I was mad they postponed.
I was voting yes.

522
00:25:20.154 --> 00:25:21.522
-You were?
-Hell yes.

523
00:25:21.589 --> 00:25:23.891
I'm ready to sell.
I'm tired of New York.

524
00:25:24.025 --> 00:25:26.660
I want to go someplace
where I'm not so damn famous.

525
00:25:26.727 --> 00:25:30.531
Maybe if you didn't walk around
in a mink coat and sunglasses.

526
00:25:30.631 --> 00:25:32.533
people wouldn't recognize you.

527
00:25:32.600 --> 00:25:33.567
Just a thought.

528
00:25:33.701 --> 00:25:38.072
I get cold. Here. Snacks.

529
00:25:38.172 --> 00:25:40.841
Ooh. yogurt-covered raisins.

530
00:25:40.941 --> 00:25:45.679
-Funny. these were missing
from the Duke's minibar.
-Yeah?

531
00:25:45.746 --> 00:25:47.681
Well. I take them
from the housekeepers' carts

532
00:25:47.782 --> 00:25:49.083
in the hallway.

533
00:25:49.183 --> 00:25:51.185
This place owes me that much.
I'm a legend.

534
00:25:51.285 --> 00:25:54.055
Yes. you are.

535
00:25:54.121 --> 00:25:55.524
But that's why
I don't understand

536
00:25:55.589 --> 00:25:57.058
why you want to leave here now.
at this point.

537
00:25:57.158 --> 00:25:59.060
Why? Because I'm gonna die soon?

538
00:25:59.126 --> 00:26:02.531
No. because you're
such a fixture here.

539
00:26:04.298 --> 00:26:06.267
This city is too run-down.

540
00:26:06.367 --> 00:26:08.269
It's like...

541
00:26:08.402 --> 00:26:10.271
everything was better
when I was younger. you know?

542
00:26:10.371 --> 00:26:14.775
Or maybe you just get to an age
where you hate everything.

543
00:26:14.909 --> 00:26:16.710
Where are you going to go?

544
00:26:16.777 --> 00:26:20.481
Someplace more elegant--
Vegas. Florida. Phoenix.

545
00:26:20.614 --> 00:26:23.784
And. uh. what about
the money you owe?

546
00:26:23.884 --> 00:26:25.052
What money?

547
00:26:25.152 --> 00:26:26.555
We happen to know
that you're in arrears

548
00:26:26.654 --> 00:26:28.389
-on your maintenance.
-Oh.

549
00:26:28.489 --> 00:26:30.559
Well. selling this place
will take care of all that

550
00:26:30.624 --> 00:26:32.893
and then some.
Now. if you don't have any more

551
00:26:32.960 --> 00:26:35.362
-questions...
-No. no. no. One more thing.

552
00:26:35.463 --> 00:26:37.998
Where were you on the night
of Duke Sebastian's murder?

553
00:26:38.099 --> 00:26:40.067
I already told your friend.

554
00:26:40.134 --> 00:26:41.769
I came up here between shows

555
00:26:41.869 --> 00:26:44.573
to watch myself in a guest role

556
00:26:44.638 --> 00:26:47.174
- on <i>Father Crime.</i>
- Woo-hoo.

557
00:26:47.308 --> 00:26:50.244
I haven't seen this week's
episode yet. What did you play?

558
00:26:50.311 --> 00:26:53.180
I played a nun in a story
about a Mother Superior.

559
00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:55.916
a murderous Mother Superior.

560
00:26:55.983 --> 00:26:57.852
I sang a song
to a bunch of orphans.

561
00:26:57.952 --> 00:26:59.521
-How did it go?
-Not bad.

562
00:26:59.653 --> 00:27:01.989
It really looked like
I was playing the guitar.

563
00:27:02.089 --> 00:27:05.493
So many shows
tell stories about nuns.

564
00:27:05.594 --> 00:27:07.795
Hair and makeup is a breeze.

565
00:27:07.862 --> 00:27:10.698
Ooh. I love index cards.
What are these for?

566
00:27:10.831 --> 00:27:13.167
Those are my contacts. I used
to keep. um. an address book

567
00:27:13.300 --> 00:27:16.203
but kept losing it.
and I use code

568
00:27:16.303 --> 00:27:18.339
so the housekeepers don't call
all my celebrity friends.

569
00:27:18.439 --> 00:27:21.709
Oh. that's smart. I love a code.

570
00:27:21.809 --> 00:27:24.378
Um. "Figaro" for my hairdresser.

571
00:27:24.478 --> 00:27:27.314
"Brontë" for my sister.
"Kid" for Michael Feinstein.

572
00:27:27.381 --> 00:27:29.350
Oh. Who's "Vernon"?

573
00:27:29.483 --> 00:27:33.320
My nephew. One of my nephews.
Grandnephew.

574
00:27:33.420 --> 00:27:35.890
And he is grand.

575
00:27:36.023 --> 00:27:37.892
That looked like
an international number.

576
00:27:37.992 --> 00:27:40.060
He works overseas. I miss him.

577
00:27:40.194 --> 00:27:43.397
I-I don't mean to be rude.
but I do have two shows tonight

578
00:27:43.532 --> 00:27:45.866
and I have to go on vocal rest.

579
00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:47.234
Thanks for your time.

580
00:27:47.368 --> 00:27:49.170
Oh. Mm.

581
00:27:49.270 --> 00:27:50.572
Bye.

582
00:27:50.671 --> 00:27:52.541
She threw you out?

583
00:27:52.673 --> 00:27:55.910
-Pretty much.
-Well. that's Ruby.

584
00:27:56.043 --> 00:28:00.814
Now. this mural is my favorite
work of art at the Hotel Reilly.

585
00:28:00.915 --> 00:28:03.717
The artist Pierre Montclair
worked on it for years.

586
00:28:03.851 --> 00:28:06.020
His daughter Ellie
still lives here.

587
00:28:06.086 --> 00:28:09.190
-You'll know it's her.
She wears Coke-bottle glasses.
-Aw.

588
00:28:09.256 --> 00:28:11.458
Apparently. it's filled
with inside jokes.

589
00:28:11.560 --> 00:28:13.528
- See the two trombone players?
- Mm-hmm.

590
00:28:13.595 --> 00:28:15.029
The Fortunato Brothers
were twins

591
00:28:15.095 --> 00:28:17.264
who played here for years.

592
00:28:17.398 --> 00:28:18.567
And that couple dancing?

593
00:28:18.699 --> 00:28:20.434
That's Harold.
a former bartender.

594
00:28:20.535 --> 00:28:23.037
and Sylvie from laundry.

595
00:28:23.103 --> 00:28:24.405
They were married
to other people.

596
00:28:24.506 --> 00:28:25.839
Oh. goodness.

597
00:28:27.274 --> 00:28:28.809
Ellie has permission
to add to the mural

598
00:28:28.909 --> 00:28:30.444
when something important
happens here.

599
00:28:30.579 --> 00:28:32.446
-That's nice.
-Mm.

600
00:28:32.581 --> 00:28:36.283
-Is Ruby Lane anywhere?
-Oh. sure. Yeah.

601
00:28:36.417 --> 00:28:38.185
She's right here.

602
00:28:38.285 --> 00:28:40.655
She told Pierre she'd have
to be carried out of here

603
00:28:40.788 --> 00:28:42.524
- feet first.
- Huh.

604
00:28:42.591 --> 00:28:44.792
That's not what she told us.

605
00:28:44.892 --> 00:28:46.894
There you are. Psst.

606
00:28:46.994 --> 00:28:50.030
My friend in bookkeeping
came through.

607
00:28:50.097 --> 00:28:52.266
Take a look at what Ruby owes.

608
00:28:52.366 --> 00:28:54.603
Oh. my Lord.

609
00:28:55.670 --> 00:28:58.272
- That's in dollars?
- A lot of them.

610
00:28:58.372 --> 00:29:00.975
Huh. And plenty of reason
for Ruby

611
00:29:01.108 --> 00:29:05.145
-to do anything to stay here.
-Mm-hmm.

612
00:29:07.982 --> 00:29:09.551
<i>Mother Abigail.</i>

613
00:29:09.651 --> 00:29:11.318
<i>Is Father Garvey right?</i>

614
00:29:11.418 --> 00:29:13.220
<i>Did you kill that pop star?</i>

615
00:29:13.320 --> 00:29:14.589
<i>Of course not.</i>

616
00:29:14.656 --> 00:29:17.024
<i>Then swear. To God.</i>

617
00:29:17.124 --> 00:29:19.793
<i>I can't.</i>

618
00:29:21.362 --> 00:29:23.497
-Huh.
-What are you watching?

619
00:29:23.632 --> 00:29:27.702
Oh. Hey. Uh. this week's episode
of <i>Father Crime.</i>

620
00:29:27.801 --> 00:29:29.136
You pay for that
streaming service?

621
00:29:29.236 --> 00:29:31.138
What? No. Just a free trial.

622
00:29:31.238 --> 00:29:32.940
So. what'd you want
to talk to me about?

623
00:29:33.007 --> 00:29:37.244
Well...
I really think that you should

624
00:29:37.344 --> 00:29:39.614
do something more romantic
when you propose to Roy.

625
00:29:39.714 --> 00:29:41.616
no matter how much
you love frozen pizza.

626
00:29:41.716 --> 00:29:43.917
I don't really want to do
a big thing.

627
00:29:44.018 --> 00:29:48.022
Yes. but you also don't want Roy
to feel disappointed.

628
00:29:48.155 --> 00:29:50.491
I knew this was gonna happen
if I told you.

629
00:29:50.592 --> 00:29:53.294
You've gotten in my head.

630
00:29:53.360 --> 00:29:54.763
Why are you doing this?

631
00:29:54.828 --> 00:29:55.996
Is it because you don't think

632
00:29:56.096 --> 00:29:57.498
I'm mature enough
to get married?

633
00:29:57.599 --> 00:30:00.434
What? No. And maturity
is not a requirement.

634
00:30:00.535 --> 00:30:02.771
You know. sometimes maybe
it's not such a bad thing

635
00:30:02.870 --> 00:30:03.971
that I'm more like Dad.

636
00:30:04.038 --> 00:30:05.272
I doubt that.

637
00:30:05.372 --> 00:30:06.508
Because Roy isn't marrying you.

638
00:30:06.608 --> 00:30:07.808
He's marrying me.

639
00:30:07.908 --> 00:30:09.476
And I don't want him
to get a false sense

640
00:30:09.544 --> 00:30:11.045
of what life with me
is going to be like. You know?

641
00:30:11.145 --> 00:30:12.714
There's gonna be more nights
of TV and pizza

642
00:30:12.846 --> 00:30:14.549
than there is flowers
and skywriting.

643
00:30:14.683 --> 00:30:16.518
Why are people
so against skywriting?

644
00:30:16.618 --> 00:30:18.018
-Chemtrails.
-Those aren't real.

645
00:30:18.152 --> 00:30:20.789
Fine. I'll stay out of it.

646
00:30:20.888 --> 00:30:21.855
Too late!

647
00:30:21.955 --> 00:30:23.223
-Hey.
-Hey.

648
00:30:23.357 --> 00:30:24.925
Hey. Teddy.

649
00:30:26.226 --> 00:30:27.194
What's going on?

650
00:30:27.294 --> 00:30:29.897
Nothing. I just...

651
00:30:32.634 --> 00:30:34.803
Do you remember how you proposed
to your wife?

652
00:30:34.902 --> 00:30:36.738
Do I remember?

653
00:30:38.472 --> 00:30:41.543
It cost me two weeks of salary.
but it was worth every penny.

654
00:30:41.643 --> 00:30:45.814
I... still have that...

655
00:30:45.879 --> 00:30:50.050
champagne cork
sitting on my dresser.

656
00:30:51.385 --> 00:30:54.922
And to this day. every year.

657
00:30:55.022 --> 00:30:57.792
on our anniversary. Claudia...

658
00:30:57.891 --> 00:31:00.562
tells the kids about that night.

659
00:31:01.862 --> 00:31:04.865
She remembers every detail.

660
00:31:04.932 --> 00:31:07.101
and so do they.

661
00:31:08.570 --> 00:31:12.574
Oh. you should see
the way her eyes...

662
00:31:12.674 --> 00:31:16.511
light up when she even

663
00:31:16.611 --> 00:31:18.278
thinks about it.

664
00:31:18.412 --> 00:31:20.815
I think I just did.

665
00:31:25.052 --> 00:31:27.154
No. I said stevia.

666
00:31:27.254 --> 00:31:28.523
Yes. ma'am.

667
00:31:29.758 --> 00:31:31.358
Hello.

668
00:31:31.425 --> 00:31:34.027
Would you. uh.
care for some company?

669
00:31:34.895 --> 00:31:35.929
Suit yourself.

670
00:31:36.029 --> 00:31:37.832
Ah. Thank you.

671
00:31:41.301 --> 00:31:44.371
I watched your episode
of <i>Father Crime.</i>

672
00:31:44.438 --> 00:31:45.573
You were very good.

673
00:31:45.673 --> 00:31:47.941
Oh. thank you.

674
00:31:48.041 --> 00:31:50.077
But I kept waiting for the song

675
00:31:50.144 --> 00:31:52.781
that you said you sang
to the orphans.

676
00:31:52.913 --> 00:31:54.783
It wasn't in the episode.

677
00:31:56.083 --> 00:31:58.385
-I guess it got cut.
-Right.

678
00:31:58.485 --> 00:32:01.756
But you said you watched it
the night of the murder.

679
00:32:03.825 --> 00:32:06.661
I'm old. I must have confused
what we shot

680
00:32:06.795 --> 00:32:07.995
and what they used.

681
00:32:08.095 --> 00:32:10.431
Sue me.
It's not out of character.

682
00:32:10.497 --> 00:32:11.965
Right.

683
00:32:12.065 --> 00:32:14.803
And you definitely
are a character.

684
00:32:15.969 --> 00:32:18.673
That must be exhausting
sometimes.

685
00:32:18.773 --> 00:32:20.542
to be the way you are. just

686
00:32:20.642 --> 00:32:25.747
to keep your place
in the city's... zeitgeist.

687
00:32:27.114 --> 00:32:31.084
I wasn't always like this.
and I wasn't always alone.

688
00:32:31.185 --> 00:32:32.854
I was married.

689
00:32:34.622 --> 00:32:37.090
Herman had a...

690
00:32:37.191 --> 00:32:38.292
...had a bagel company.

691
00:32:38.358 --> 00:32:40.327
We had a lot of laughs.

692
00:32:41.261 --> 00:32:42.831
He died too young
and left me enough

693
00:32:42.963 --> 00:32:45.098
to buy that dump upstairs.

694
00:32:45.966 --> 00:32:48.368
After that. I realized...

695
00:32:48.503 --> 00:32:50.170
the only way to survive

696
00:32:50.270 --> 00:32:53.508
being an older woman
in this city

697
00:32:53.608 --> 00:32:57.344
is to be... memorable.

698
00:32:58.880 --> 00:33:01.148
Not just yesterday's news.

699
00:33:04.218 --> 00:33:06.821
Oh. it's. um...

700
00:33:06.888 --> 00:33:08.155
nice chatting with you.

701
00:33:08.222 --> 00:33:10.725
I have a hair appointment.

702
00:33:10.859 --> 00:33:14.228
See you around. Elsbeth.

703
00:33:26.774 --> 00:33:29.476
That sounds familiar.

704
00:33:29.577 --> 00:33:31.411
It's "September Song."

705
00:33:40.187 --> 00:33:43.758
- For me. it's a long while
from May to October.

706
00:33:43.892 --> 00:33:45.960
It's from a show called
<i>Knickerbocker Holiday.</i>

707
00:33:46.059 --> 00:33:48.495
Oh. What was the show about?

708
00:33:48.596 --> 00:33:51.331
It was a musical comedy
about fascism.

709
00:33:51.431 --> 00:33:53.233
It wasn't very popular.

710
00:33:53.367 --> 00:33:56.236
but it gets revived
every once in a while.

711
00:33:56.336 --> 00:34:01.174
The music is so beautiful...
and sad.

712
00:34:01.275 --> 00:34:02.544
Kurt Weill.

713
00:34:02.644 --> 00:34:04.879
He wrote it a few years
after fleeing the Nazis.

714
00:34:04.979 --> 00:34:07.882
He figured people
are safe in America.

715
00:34:07.949 --> 00:34:10.585
Maybe that's why
it sounds so sad.

716
00:34:11.451 --> 00:34:15.690
So the Great American Songbook
isn't just American. is it?

717
00:34:15.790 --> 00:34:17.424
Oh. no. Some of our
best composers

718
00:34:17.525 --> 00:34:20.160
came through Ellis Island
on the way to Tin Pan Alley.

719
00:34:20.260 --> 00:34:22.362
Irving Berlin. Vernon Duke.

720
00:34:22.429 --> 00:34:24.532
-Igor Stravinsky.
-Wait.

721
00:34:24.632 --> 00:34:26.133
Vernon Duke?

722
00:34:27.735 --> 00:34:31.940
"Vernon" was on the index card
above Ruby's bed.

723
00:34:32.040 --> 00:34:35.142
It must've been code
for Duke Sebastian.

724
00:34:35.275 --> 00:34:38.178
whom she claimed she didn't
know. She definitely knew him.

725
00:34:38.278 --> 00:34:39.948
Jolly. was there
anything different

726
00:34:40.080 --> 00:34:41.716
about the second show
that night?

727
00:34:41.783 --> 00:34:44.451
Was Ruby distracted
or out of breath?

728
00:34:44.552 --> 00:34:47.120
She seemed happy.
which was different.

729
00:34:47.254 --> 00:34:48.957
Oh. and she changed
that one bit.

730
00:34:49.057 --> 00:34:51.726
-What bit?
-She didn't take out
her Mabel Short Award.

731
00:34:51.793 --> 00:34:53.160
She said she forgot to bring it.

732
00:34:53.293 --> 00:34:55.730
but when I placed
the tote bag onstage.

733
00:34:55.797 --> 00:34:57.899
it weighed the same as always.

734
00:34:59.767 --> 00:35:01.636
Like Ruby said. "The lights.

735
00:35:01.736 --> 00:35:06.975
the proximity
expose everything."

736
00:35:55.056 --> 00:35:58.059
Hey. do you mind?
I'm working.

737
00:35:58.158 --> 00:36:00.128
Ruby Lane. you're under arrest
for the murder

738
00:36:00.193 --> 00:36:03.330
of Duke Sebastian Mason.
Lord of Sussexford.

739
00:36:03.397 --> 00:36:06.067
- That's absurd.
- It did seem so.

740
00:36:06.199 --> 00:36:10.004
but we now know you owe
this hotel so much money.

741
00:36:10.071 --> 00:36:13.407
you could not let the Duke vote
to approve the sale.

742
00:36:13.541 --> 00:36:15.208
So you lied.

743
00:36:15.342 --> 00:36:17.679
pretending you didn't know him.
but you had

744
00:36:17.779 --> 00:36:19.379
his phone number taped
to your wall.

745
00:36:19.514 --> 00:36:21.582
We found it in your trash.

746
00:36:21.716 --> 00:36:24.384
And your alibi was shaky.

747
00:36:24.519 --> 00:36:27.722
You pretended that you watched
your episode of <i>Father Crime.</i>

748
00:36:27.822 --> 00:36:30.725
but you didn't know
they'd cut your song.

749
00:36:30.858 --> 00:36:32.560
And don't forget
the yogurt-covered raisins

750
00:36:32.694 --> 00:36:33.728
from the minibar.

751
00:36:33.828 --> 00:36:35.362
How could we?

752
00:36:35.462 --> 00:36:37.899
All that proves nothing.
I'm old.

753
00:36:37.999 --> 00:36:41.703
drunk. outrageous.
I get confused.

754
00:36:41.803 --> 00:36:44.072
No. I don't think you do.

755
00:36:44.172 --> 00:36:48.076
See. I think you pretend
to drink and forget your lyrics

756
00:36:48.176 --> 00:36:51.779
-to make yourself memorable.
-It's working.

757
00:36:51.879 --> 00:36:53.346
But you're right.
it is all

758
00:36:53.447 --> 00:36:55.248
circumstantial
without the murder weapon.

759
00:36:55.382 --> 00:36:58.953
Now. I figured that
you used the shoehorn

760
00:36:59.087 --> 00:37:00.621
from the Duke's closet

761
00:37:00.722 --> 00:37:03.323
to pry off the towel bar
and then make it look like a...

762
00:37:03.423 --> 00:37:05.193
slip-and-fall accident.

763
00:37:05.292 --> 00:37:07.762
But the Duke's room
wasn't as decrepit as yours.

764
00:37:07.862 --> 00:37:11.431
so you had to find something
heavy to use as a hammer.

765
00:37:11.532 --> 00:37:14.569
Like that award
you carry around.

766
00:37:14.635 --> 00:37:17.270
-Can anyone follow this?
-It's not that hard.

767
00:37:17.370 --> 00:37:21.109
But the base of your
Mabel Short Award is.

768
00:37:21.209 --> 00:37:23.811
After you used it
to kill the Duke

769
00:37:23.911 --> 00:37:28.015
and then hammer off the
towel bar. you came back here...

770
00:37:28.950 --> 00:37:31.652
...to the second show.
only this time

771
00:37:31.753 --> 00:37:33.386
you cut the bit
where you take the award

772
00:37:33.487 --> 00:37:37.125
out of your tote. which. um.
really upset Jolly.

773
00:37:37.257 --> 00:37:39.127
I'm a creature of habit
and professionalism.

774
00:37:39.227 --> 00:37:42.230
You didn't take it out
because you realized

775
00:37:42.295 --> 00:37:45.733
the stage lights and the
proximity to the audience

776
00:37:45.800 --> 00:37:49.302
would have exposed any blood
or other evidence.

777
00:37:49.436 --> 00:37:51.806
so you waited
until after the show

778
00:37:51.939 --> 00:37:54.441
where you could
wash it all away.

779
00:37:54.542 --> 00:37:57.011
-That is a wild accusation.
-Yes. but

780
00:37:57.145 --> 00:38:00.280
we can prove it. All we need
to do is examine your award.

781
00:38:00.347 --> 00:38:01.783
Well. you can't.

782
00:38:01.883 --> 00:38:04.384
You don't have a warrant.

783
00:38:04.484 --> 00:38:06.486
And this is mine.

784
00:38:06.587 --> 00:38:09.824
See. I told you

785
00:38:09.924 --> 00:38:13.728
I do have a tote
just like that one.

786
00:38:13.828 --> 00:38:16.429
Oh. dear. did I take
the wrong one?

787
00:38:16.496 --> 00:38:18.866
You may have washed it off.

788
00:38:19.000 --> 00:38:21.368
but there. clear as day.

789
00:38:21.501 --> 00:38:26.007
are the imprints
from the shoehorn handle.

790
00:38:27.440 --> 00:38:30.111
They really do monogram
everything at this hotel.

791
00:38:30.178 --> 00:38:31.746
It's like stepping back in time.

792
00:38:31.846 --> 00:38:35.783
It looks like the gig
is finally up.

793
00:38:36.684 --> 00:38:38.753
You have the right
to remain silent.

794
00:38:38.853 --> 00:38:41.388
That's all right.
I know the words.

795
00:38:41.488 --> 00:38:43.558
See. I knew you were lying
when you said

796
00:38:43.691 --> 00:38:44.959
you wanted to leave New York.

797
00:38:45.026 --> 00:38:47.295
How could anybody want to leave?

798
00:38:47.394 --> 00:38:49.462
Feet first.

799
00:38:53.167 --> 00:38:56.403
I wonder what's gonna happen
to this place.

800
00:39:01.976 --> 00:39:05.213
I don't understand. The sign
said the place is closed.

801
00:39:05.313 --> 00:39:07.081
Yeah. for a private event.

802
00:39:07.215 --> 00:39:09.050
That's us.

803
00:39:14.188 --> 00:39:15.488
Shall I bring the first course?

804
00:39:15.590 --> 00:39:16.824
Not yet.

805
00:39:16.891 --> 00:39:19.527
I'm gonna do something
before I lose my nerve.

806
00:39:19.594 --> 00:39:21.428
What are you...?

807
00:39:21.529 --> 00:39:24.932
Oh. my God.

808
00:39:56.764 --> 00:39:59.033
It's true.

809
00:39:59.133 --> 00:40:01.102
He could be on Broadway.

810
00:40:01.202 --> 00:40:02.770
Right?

811
00:40:20.420 --> 00:40:23.624
I still have the cork
sitting on my dresser.

812
00:40:23.758 --> 00:40:26.260
Teddy's getting married.

813
00:40:26.327 --> 00:40:27.494
Yeah.

814
00:40:27.595 --> 00:40:28.763
It happened so fast.

815
00:40:28.863 --> 00:40:31.299
Hey. You okay?

816
00:40:31.431 --> 00:40:32.533
Yeah. I'm okay.

817
00:40:32.633 --> 00:40:34.835
-Oh.
-It's just the end of one season

818
00:40:34.936 --> 00:40:36.170
and the beginning of another.

819
00:40:37.605 --> 00:40:38.839
I know things will change.

820
00:40:38.973 --> 00:40:40.508
and maybe they'll move
to the suburbs.

821
00:40:40.641 --> 00:40:42.576
but no matter what...

822
00:40:42.643 --> 00:40:45.746
I'm not gonna miss out
on being a grandmother

823
00:40:45.813 --> 00:40:47.682
because my clothes are so soft

824
00:40:47.815 --> 00:40:49.984
-and they're gonna love my hugs.
-Hey.

825
00:40:50.117 --> 00:40:52.653
Slow down.

826
00:40:52.753 --> 00:40:55.089
You have nothing to worry about.

827
00:40:55.189 --> 00:40:56.424
Teddy's a mama's boy.

828
00:40:56.489 --> 00:40:59.894
And you are never gonna change.

829
00:40:59.994 --> 00:41:01.662
That's why people love you.

830
00:41:05.032 --> 00:41:07.601
It's gonna be hard
to leave this place.

831
00:41:07.702 --> 00:41:09.403
For me. too.

832
00:41:09.503 --> 00:41:12.640
I was just getting used
to Deandra.

833
00:41:12.707 --> 00:41:15.142
They like me so much.
I'm being promoted

834
00:41:15.209 --> 00:41:17.311
to work for the
Havenford Group office.

835
00:41:17.378 --> 00:41:20.014
Captain Kershaw thinks
it's a smart move.

836
00:41:20.147 --> 00:41:23.150
I kind of like it when you
pop up in surprising places.

837
00:41:23.217 --> 00:41:27.388
-Hmm.
-Hey. Did you ever figure out
who was trying to buy the hotel?

838
00:41:28.222 --> 00:41:30.224
Hmm.

839
00:42:26.580 --> 00:42:28.517
Hey. did you see that?

840
00:42:28.582 --> 00:42:31.185
Oh. I love that show.
Wasn't it wonderful?

841
00:42:31.285 --> 00:42:33.154
It was so great. I cried.

842
00:42:33.254 --> 00:42:35.289
Yeah. me. too.

843
00:42:35.389 --> 00:42:38.192
Isn't it great to love things?
